<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Entitlements for the BUNDLED `node` in Contents/Resources/sidecar/node.

    The app spawns it as a child process, and a child process gets its own code
    signature and its own entitlements — the app's Entitlements.plist does not
    extend to it. Under the hardened runtime (which notarization requires) an
    unentitled node dies as soon as V8 allocates executable memory, which from
    the UI looks like the sidecar simply failing to start.

    This is deliberately the *same set the official Node.js macOS build signs
    itself with*, minus one entry. Re-signing replaces entitlements wholesale,
    so trimming anything upstream Node considers necessary risks a failure that
    only ever shows up in a packaged build — the worst kind to debug here.

    The omitted entry is `com.apple.security.get-task-allow`, which upstream
    Node does ship. It lets a debugger attach, and Apple's notary service
    rejects any binary carrying it. Dropping it is mandatory, not a preference.
    scripts/sign-desktop.sh asserts it is gone before submitting.

    No microphone here: that is the WKWebView in the app process, not node.
  -->

  <!-- V8's JIT. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- node loads native addons (.node) and their dylibs — onnxruntime, sharp,
       canvas — and resolves some of them through DYLD_ variables. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
